O.NCCCCCN","substrate_name":null,"claims":[{"claim":"The required reaction direction is formation.","basis":"reaction verb in the target description","implication":"a catalyst whose native reaction runs the opposite direction is disqualified","status":"derived_from_target","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"The target inputs have free_small_molecule topology.","basis":"structure of the named substrates","implication":"a catalyst requiring a different substrate topology may not accept these inputs","status":"derived_from_target","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"The target substrates carry these reactive groups: carboxyl, primary_amine.","basis":"functional groups detected in the substrate structures","implication":"the catalytic chemistry must engage one or more of these groups","status":"derived_from_target","uncertainty":"low"},{"claim":"Direct condensation of a carboxylate with an ammonium/amine is thermodynamically near-neutral to unfavorable in water and kinetically blocked, because the carboxylate is a poor electrophile and the protonated amine is a poor nucleophile. The carboxyl must first be converted to a better acyl donor, or the reaction driven by coupling to a favorable process.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"the catalyst must resolve this barrier","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"Plausible activation strategies: acyl-adenylate (acyl-AMP) from ATP, with PPi release, acyl-phosphate from ATP, with ADP release, acyl-CoA thioester (CoA ligation, then aminolysis), covalent acyl-enzyme intermediate (ester or thioester) formed from an activated donor, then aminolysis, reverse-hydrolysis (kinetically controlled or equilibrium-shifted) under low water activity or product removal, ATP-grasp-type or other nucleotide-dependent acid-amine ligation.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"a genuine candidate should realize one of these strategies","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"A cofactor or leaving group is chemically implied: ATP with Mg2+ (leaving groups AMP + PPi, or ADP + Pi).","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"the catalyst likely requires this cofactor/leaving group","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"A cofactor or leaving group is chemically implied: or CoA (and ATP) if proceeding via an acyl-CoA thioester.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"the catalyst likely requires this cofactor/leaving group","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"A cofactor or leaving group is chemically implied: no cofactor if an ATP-independent acyl-transfer or reverse-hydrolysis mechanism is used.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"the catalyst likely requires this cofactor/leaving group","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"Disqualifying observation: Candidate acts only on monocarboxylic or long-chain fatty acid substrates and has a pocket that cannot accommodate the C5 dicarboxylate with its distal charged carboxylate.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"a candidate showing this is ruled out","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"Disqualifying observation: Candidate acts only on alpha-amino acids or alpha-amines and requires an alpha-carboxylate/alpha-amino recognition motif absent in the aliphatic diamine.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"a candidate showing this is ruled out","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"Disqualifying observation: Candidate acts only on carrier-protein-tethered or macromolecular acyl donors or acceptors, with no evidence of free small-molecule turnover.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"a candidate showing this is ruled out","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"Disqualifying observation: Candidate is a hydrolase or protease with no evidence of net synthesis or transacylation and no way to overcome hydrolytic equilibrium.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"a candidate showing this is ruled out","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"Disqualifying observation: Candidate has no ability to bind a linear diamine with a protonatable distal amine, or requires an acceptor that is a secondary or aromatic amine.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"a candidate showing this is ruled out","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"Disqualifying observation: Candidate is a transferase that moves the acyl group only to water, an alcohol, or a thiol, never to a primary amine.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"a candidate showing this is ruled out","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"Disqualifying observation: No plausible acyl-activation chemistry (no nucleotide, CoA, or nucleophilic active-site residue).","basis":"mechanistic reasoning from the extracted facts","implication":"a candidate showing this is ruled out","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"},{"claim":"The target is a repeating chain (-[NH-(CH2)5-NH-C(=O)-(CH2)3-C(=O)]- (glutaroyl-cadaverine amide repeat, nylon-5,5 type)); a representative oligomer of about 6 residues should stand in for the chain in structural modeling, not a single monomer or dimer.","basis":"mechanistic reasoning about the target's polymeric context","implication":"structural modeling should use the derived oligomer as the substrate","status":"inferred","uncertainty":"medium"}],"extraction_disagreements":[],"reaction_direction":"formation","bond_types":["amide C-N bond (carboxylic acid + primary amine -> secondary amide, with loss of water)"],"substrate_topology":"free_small_molecule","reactive_groups":["carboxyl","primary_amine"],"activation_problem":"Direct condensation of a carboxylate with an ammonium/amine is thermodynamically near-neutral to unfavorable in water and kinetically blocked, because the carboxylate is a poor electrophile and the protonated amine is a poor nucleophile. The carboxyl must first be converted to a better acyl donor, or the reaction driven by coupling to a favorable process.","activation_categories":["acyl-adenylate (acyl-AMP) from ATP, with PPi release","acyl-phosphate from ATP, with ADP release","acyl-CoA thioester (CoA ligation, then aminolysis)","covalent acyl-enzyme intermediate (ester or thioester) formed from an activated donor, then aminolysis","reverse-hydrolysis (kinetically controlled or equilibrium-shifted) under low water activity or product removal","ATP-grasp-type or other nucleotide-dependent acid-amine ligation"],"cofactor_requirements":["ATP with Mg2+ (leaving groups AMP + PPi, or ADP + Pi)","or CoA (and ATP) if proceeding via an acyl-CoA thioester","no cofactor if an ATP-independent acyl-transfer or reverse-hydrolysis mechanism is used"],"disqualifiers":["Candidate acts only on monocarboxylic or long-chain fatty acid substrates and has a pocket that cannot accommodate the C5 dicarboxylate with its distal charged carboxylate","Candidate acts only on alpha-amino acids or alpha-amines and requires an alpha-carboxylate/alpha-amino recognition motif absent in the aliphatic diamine","Candidate acts only on carrier-protein-tethered or macromolecular acyl donors or acceptors, with no evidence of free small-molecule turnover","Candidate is a hydrolase or protease with no evidence of net synthesis or transacylation and no way to overcome hydrolytic equilibrium","Candidate has no ability to bind a linear diamine with a protonatable distal amine, or requires an acceptor that is a secondary or aromatic amine","Candidate is a transferase that moves the acyl group only to water, an alcohol, or a thiol, never to a primary amine","No plausible acyl-activation chemistry (no nucleotide, CoA, or nucleophilic active-site residue)"],"polymer_target":true,"modeling_oligomer":{"smiles":"NCCCCCNC(=O)CCCC(=O)NCCCCCNC(=O)CCCC(=O)NCCCCCNC(=O)CCCC(=O)O","n_backbone_bonds":5,"n_residues_min":4,"basis":"llm_derived","repeat_unit":"-[NH-(CH2)5-NH-C(=O)-(CH2)3-C(=O)]- (glutaroyl-cadaverine amide repeat, nylon-5,5 type)"},"modeling_specification":{"state":"Pre-reactive Michaelis-type complex for amide-bond formation, with the acid component as its carboxylate (the form activated by ATP, or the acyl donor in the aminolysis step) and the neutral, unprotonated primary amine positioned for nucleophilic attack on the activated carbonyl carbon.","components":[{"source_component_index":1,"name":"glutarate (pentanedioic acid)","role":"acyl donor (carboxylate that is activated, then attacked by the amine)","modeling_smiles":"[O-]C(=O)CCCC(=O)[O-]","state_rationale":"The source SMILES is the neutral diacid; at physiological pH both carboxyls (pKa ~4.3 and ~5.4) are deprotonated, and the carboxylate oxygen is the nucleophile toward ATP alpha-phosphate or the acyl-activation partner. Glutarate has no stereocenters. The change from neutral acid to dianion is flagged for review.","reactive_atom":"carbonyl carbon of one carboxylate (C1 or C5; the two are symmetry-equivalent in free glutarate)","include_in_model":true,"affinity_target":true},{"source_component_index":2,"name":"cadaverine (pentane-1,5-diamine)","role":"nucleophile (amine attacking the activated acyl carbon)","modeling_smiles":"NCCCCCN","state_rationale":"The source SMILES is the neutral free base. The reacting amine must be an unprotonated lone-pair nucleophile; the distal amine may be protonated (-NH3+) in the bound state, depending on the pocket. The neutral form is modeled for the attacking amine, and this assumption is flagged for review. Cadaverine has no stereocenters.","reactive_atom":"terminal primary amine nitrogen (N1) of one end of the chain","include_in_model":true,"affinity_target":true}],"geometry":{"kind":"distance_angle","atom_a":"attacking amine nitrogen of cadaverine","atom_b":"carboxyl/acyl carbon of glutarate (activated carbonyl carbon)","atom_c":"carbonyl oxygen of the same glutarate acyl group","rationale":"Nucleophilic addition requires the N...C(=O) distance to be about 3 A or less, with an approach angle near the Burgi-Dunitz trajectory (about 105-110 degrees) at the carbonyl carbon, to form the tetrahedral intermediate."},"uncertainties":["The activation route (acyl-AMP, acyl-CoA, acyl-enzyme, or reverse hydrolysis) is not specified by the reaction description, so the modeled acyl state may need to be an adenylate, thioester, or tetrahedral intermediate instead of the free carboxylate","Protonation states: glutarate modeled as the dianion and the attacking amine of cadaverine as a neutral free base, which differ from the source neutral acid and may need review","It is unclear whether the target is a single mono-amide (N-(5-aminopentyl)glutaramic acid) or a polyamide (nylon-5,5-type oligomer); both substrates are bifunctional","Which carboxyl of glutarate and which amine of cadaverine react is symmetry-degenerate in the free substrates, and the recognition of the distal charged group is unknown"]}}},"target_inputs":[{"id":"T1","smiles":"O=C(O)CCCC(=O)O"},{"id":"T2","smiles":"NCCCCCN"}]}

